<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ApprovalProcess xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS COMMENT SITS INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT DELIBERATELY. A comment placed ABOVE
        <ApprovalProcess> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with a misleading
        "unable to find matching parent xml file" error. The rationale below lives in this comment
        rather than in <description> because a comment is repo-only and is never sent to the
        Metadata API. ⚠ DO NOT ASSUME THE 255-CHARACTER CAP that binds RecordType and
        ValidationRule descriptions applies here: the five approval files already in this repo
        carry descriptions of 442 to 617 characters (measured against git HEAD, 2026-08-19).
        The descriptions written in this change are nevertheless kept under 255, so the rule is
        uniform across every metadata type in this tranche and never has to be re-measured.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 RETRACTED 2026-08-19 - THIS PROCESS NOW CARRIES A FINAL APPROVAL ACTION.
        =====================================================================================
        The superseded sentence is quoted and retracted rather than deleted, because it is the
        claim a future reader is most likely to reason from:

            RETRACTED: "No field updates fire on approval or rejection - approval history
            records who and when, and the stage is advanced by a human afterwards."

        WHAT REPLACED IT: the client's redesign makes the approval OWN the transition. Approval
        now fires the Set_Approval_Advance_Pending field update, which is a SEMAPHORE, not a
        stage write.

        🔴 THE OBJECTION BEHIND THE RETRACTED SENTENCE IS ANSWERED, NOT IGNORED. That sentence
        existed because an approval FieldUpdate BYPASSES VALIDATION RULES ENTIRELY - the same
        argument Wire_Complete_Before_Completed's comment made. It is still true, which is
        exactly why the field update writes a FLAG and not Disposition_Stage__c. The flag
        re-fires DispositionTrigger; the after-update handler enqueues
        DispositionApprovalAdvanceQueueable, which writes the stage with ordinary
        Database.update(..., false, AccessLevel.SYSTEM_MODE). Validation rules DO evaluate
        against that write. An approval-triggered FLOW was rejected separately: with no
        runInMode it runs as the APPROVER, a measured incident on this org.

        WHAT THE ADVANCE TARGETS. Readiness is the one hop whose target depends on the record
        type: On_Market goes to BOV Outreach, Off_Market goes to Broker Selection. The Queueable
        derives that from (record type, current stage), which is why ONE semaphore serves all
        three Disposition-target approvals - their entry stages (Disposition Readiness,
        Broker Selection, Closing) are mutually exclusive, so there is nothing to disambiguate.

        ⚠ IF THE ADVANCE FAILS the flag is left TRUE, on purpose: the record is findable by
        SOQL and recoverable. It is not visible on any page - Approval_Advance_Pending__c
        deliberately has no FLS anywhere. See that field's own XML comment.

        SUBMISSION IS NOW AUTOMATIC. DispositionService.initiateAndSubmit creates the record at
        Disposition Readiness and submits it in the same transaction, from the Sell Meter
        Initiate modal. A manual resubmit route survives as the Submit_Sale_Decision quick
        action. allowRecall stays true and recordEditability stays AdminOnly - the AdminOnly
        lock releases before the Queueable runs, which is why the asynchronous hop is safe.

        DEPLOY ORDER - LOAD-BEARING. workflows/Disposition__c.workflow-meta.xml must land FIRST
        or this file fails on an unknown field update. DispositionTriggerHandler's semaphore hook
        should be live before this lands in a real org, or an approval will set a flag nothing
        reads.

        =====================================================================================
        2026-08-21 - THREE NEW ELEMENTS, PLUS A SECOND ACTION ON finalApprovalActions.
        THIS IS THE FILE THE UNPROVEN SHAPE IS BEING PROVEN ON. READ ALL OF THIS.
        =====================================================================================
        WHAT CHANGED: initialSubmissionActions (new), finalRejectionActions (new, this file had
        none), recallActions (new), and Clear_Approval_Pending appended as a SECOND action inside
        the EXISTING finalApprovalActions. Set_Approval_Advance_Pending is untouched and stays
        FIRST; the D-1 semaphore must keep firing exactly as before.

        WHAT IT IS FOR: hiding the Submit_Sale_Decision quick action on Disposition_Record_Page
        while this approval is genuinely pending. The four actions write and clear
        Disposition__c.Approval_Pending__c, which four Dynamic Actions on that page read. The
        server side guard in DispositionApprovalService is UNCHANGED and remains the
        authoritative check: this is a UX affordance layered over a working refusal.

        🔴 initialSubmissionActions AND recallActions APPEAR NOWHERE ELSE IN THIS REPO. Before
        this change, a grep across all seven files in force-app/main/default/approvalProcesses/
        returned ZERO occurrences of either. Only finalApprovalActions and finalRejectionActions
        were proven here. What the shape below rests on, stated as evidence rather than assumed:
          1. The Metadata API ApprovalProcess reference types BOTH new elements as
             ApprovalAction, the SAME type as finalApprovalActions and finalRejectionActions,
             and defines ApprovalAction as one repeating field `action` of type
             WorkflowActionReference[]. So the child shape below is not a guess; it is the same
             child shape already deployed in this repo twice.
          2. Element ORDER in these files is alphabetical. That was an observation about five
             files; the Metadata API reference presents the type's fields in the same
             alphabetical order, which corroborates it independently. initialSubmissionActions
             therefore sits after finalRejectionRecordLock and before label; recallActions after
             processOrder and before recordEditability.

        🔴 A GREEN DEPLOY IS NOT PROOF THAT recallActions FIRES, AND THE FAILURE MODE IS THE
        DANGEROUS DIRECTION. A recallActions block that deploys and never executes leaves
        Approval_Pending__c stuck TRUE on a record that is no longer locked and no longer has an
        approval, hiding its Submit button PERMANENTLY with no route to resubmit and nothing
        logged anywhere. That is strictly worse than the noisy refusal this change replaces.
        REQUIRED BEHAVIOURAL READBACK, on this process alone, before the other three are
        deployed:
            1. submit a test disposition at Disposition Readiness;
            2. confirm Approval_Pending__c reads TRUE and the Submit button is gone;
            3. RECALL it from the Approval History component in the page header;
            4. confirm Approval_Pending__c reads FALSE and the button is back.
        If step 4 fails, revert this file rather than substituting another mechanism; the
        alternatives were analysed and rejected, and the safe fallback is today's behaviour.

        WHY RECALL IS NOT A HYPOTHETICAL HERE. allowRecall stays true, recordEditability stays
        AdminOnly, and the Approval History component was added to Disposition_Record_Page on
        2026-08-19 (code review C-3) SPECIFICALLY so the Recall button stopped being unreachable
        from the UI. Recall is now a routine one click operation on this page, so a design that
        left the button hidden after one would break a workflow that change went out of its way
        to enable. recallActions is the ONLY hook for the event: a recall fires no rejection
        action, leaves no ProcessInstance status the record can read, and has no trigger context.

        ⚠ THE REQUIREMENT IS "HIDDEN WHILE PENDING", NOT "HIDDEN UNTIL REJECTED". The flag's
        lifetime is exactly the ProcessInstance.Status = 'Pending' window, and all four terminal
        transitions clear it. That is the deliberate difference from a flag whose clearing
        depends on one specific exit path.

        ⚠ TWO SIMILARLY NAMED FIELDS ARE NOW WRITTEN BY THIS FILE. Approval_Advance_Pending__c
        (the D-1 semaphore, true on FINAL APPROVAL) and Approval_Pending__c (true while PENDING).
        They are opposites in time. Read the banner on
        objects/Disposition__c/fields/Approval_Pending__c.field-meta.xml before editing either.

        DEPLOY ORDER for this change: objects/Disposition__c/fields/Approval_Pending__c
        .field-meta.xml, then workflows/Disposition__c.workflow-meta.xml, then this file, then
        the two Disposition permission sets, then the FlexiPage LAST. Reversing the last two
        hides all four Submit buttons for everyone, silently.
    -->
    <active>true</active>
    <allowRecall>true</allowRecall>
    <allowedSubmitters>
        <type>owner</type>
    </allowedSubmitters>
    <approvalPageFields>
        <field>Name</field>
        <field>Owner</field>
        <field>Disposition_Stage__c</field>
        <field>Property_Asset__c</field>
        <field>Sell_Decision_Trigger__c</field>
    </approvalPageFields>
    <approvalStep>
        <allowDelegate>false</allowDelegate>
        <assignedApprover>
            <approver>
                <name>nikhil.dhanani@usmandpeg.uat</name>
                <type>user</type>
            </approver>
            <approver>
                <name>aftab.ali.dpeg.usman@avanzasolutions.com</name>
                <type>user</type>
            </approver>
            <whenMultipleApprovers>FirstResponse</whenMultipleApprovers>
        </assignedApprover>
        <label>Principal Review</label>
        <name>Principal_Review_Step</name>
    </approvalStep>
    <description>Principal sign-off on the decision to sell. Either principal decides, first response wins. Entry: stage is Disposition Readiness. Approval fires the Set_Approval_Advance_Pending semaphore; a Queueable then advances the stage. See this file&apos;s XML comment.</description>
    <enableMobileDeviceAccess>false</enableMobileDeviceAccess>
    <entryCriteria>
        <criteriaItems>
            <field>Disposition__c.Disposition_Stage__c</field>
            <operation>equals</operation>
            <value>Disposition Readiness</value>
        </criteriaItems>
    </entryCriteria>
    <finalApprovalActions>
        <action>
            <name>Set_Approval_Advance_Pending</name>
            <type>FieldUpdate</type>
        </action>
        <action>
            <name>Clear_Approval_Pending</name>
            <type>FieldUpdate</type>
        </action>
    </finalApprovalActions>
    <finalApprovalRecordLock>false</finalApprovalRecordLock>
    <finalRejectionActions>
        <action>
            <name>Clear_Approval_Pending</name>
            <type>FieldUpdate</type>
        </action>
    </finalRejectionActions>
    <finalRejectionRecordLock>false</finalRejectionRecordLock>
    <initialSubmissionActions>
        <action>
            <name>Set_Approval_Pending</name>
            <type>FieldUpdate</type>
        </action>
    </initialSubmissionActions>
    <label>Sale Decision Approval</label>
    <processOrder>1</processOrder>
    <recallActions>
        <action>
            <name>Clear_Approval_Pending</name>
            <type>FieldUpdate</type>
        </action>
    </recallActions>
    <recordEditability>AdminOnly</recordEditability>
    <showApprovalHistory>true</showApprovalHistory>
</ApprovalProcess>
